effects. Defining the struct fields... Defining the default shrimp parameters with standard values for temperature ranges, reproduction rates, egg development timing, and juvenile vulnerability thresholds. Continuing with the struct definitions and adding new event causes, error types, and process parameters for the shrimp simulation system. Setting default values for the shrimp and microfauna parameters, then updating the state and snapshot structures to track juvenile counts and berried females alongside the new runtime parameters and stability metrics. Writing the shrimp stress calculation... Still writing stress calculations...\n\nNow I'm moving into the daily shrimp lifecycle update, which runs after the microfauna turnover. I need to check if there are any shrimp in the tank first—if the population is zero, I'll just reset the hourly accumulators and return early. I also need to validate that the water volume is valid before proceeding. Then I'm handling the shrimp feeding logic.\n\nContinuing through the daily updates...\n\nNow I'm checking if molt stress is high enough to trigger a warning, and if so, I'm calculating the specific causes—checking mineral levels by converting calcium and magnesium concentrations to general hardness, then flagging low minerals, water instability, or temperature stress as contributing factors. Finishing the feeding calculation, I'm determining how much periphyton the shrimp can consume based on their population and feeding rate, capping it at half the available biomass per day. Now I'm moving into the condition update logic.\n\nComputing water quality parameters by converting total nutrient amounts to concentrations, calculating ammonia speciation based on pH and temperature, and deriving general hardness from calcium and magnesium levels.\n\nNow I'm factoring in food availability from periphyton relative to shrimp population, along with dissolved oxygen and ammonia stress factors that will influence growth rates.\n\nCalculating