oth runs should follow identical code paths and produce identical results. The real risk is the water_change_mass_balance test, since the microfauna boost affects decomposer rates and TAN levels, which could throw off the mass balance calculations.\n\nLet me think through what that test actually does. It probably sets up some initial state, performs a 50% water change with zero nutrients, then verifies that each dissolved pool drops by exactly 50% ± 0.5%. The nitrogen cycle changes would happen before the water change in both runs, so they'd be the same. The 50% drop should still hold because water changes are a direct fraction of current totals, regardless of what those totals are.\n\nThe microfauna boost might shift values if the test runs for hours before the water change, but since the test checks the ratio of change rather than absolute values, it should still pass as long as the water change logic itself hasn't changed.\n\nNow I need to update the ProcessParamsPreset struct and default.toml to include the new shrimp and microfauna process parameters, and make sure the process_preset_to_params function in scenarios/lib.rs handles them correctly. Let me start implementing the full set of changes.\n\nHere's my implementation plan: adding accumulators and stability tracking to biology.rs, new event causes and action types, shrimp and microfauna process parameters, expanded tank state fields, new systems modules for shrimp and microfauna behavior, nitrogen cycle updates, wiring everything through the engine, adding invariants, and expanding the presets and TOML configuration files.\n\nNow I need to materialize the shrimp data in scenarios and add comprehensive tests, but first I should check the tank_data loading pattern to ensure I'm following the right conventions for how parameters get deserialized and converted.\n\nI'm settling on putting `ShrimpRuntimeParams` and `StabilityTracker` in `biology.rs` since they're used by the daily update logic, then adding them as fields to `TankState`. For backward compatibility with existing TOML files, the new fields in `ProcessParams` will automatically get default values through the existing struct-level `#[serde(default)]` attribute.\n\nHowever, `AnimalState` doesn't have that struct-level attribute, so I'll need to add `#[serde(default)]` individually to each new field there.\n\nNow I'm ready to start implementing this across the files. I should also make sure `ProcessParamsPreset` gets the new fields and update the default configuration file for completeness, then check if there's a `load_shrimp` function in the tank data module.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2595,"cache_read_input_tokens":78539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2595},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24615e58-d50c-4dac-86b6-bdc30a050fb6","uuid":"3994f17e-ae5e-4992-b835-481716214d6d"}
