ritus density. In the overfed tank scenario with 10g of fine detritus in 30L, the density works out to 0.33 g/L, which maps to a resource factor of 0.67 using the current 0.5 reference point—that's quite high and likely driving excessive microfauna proliferation. I should increase that reference density threshold to 2.0 to make the microfauna less responsive to the extra detritus in the overfed condition.\n\nAlternatively, I could slow down the population growth rate itself by adjusting the smoothing parameter, though I'm also noticing the base growth state initializes both population and grazing pressure indices at 0.1, which might be where I need to make adjustments instead.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, the real issue is that my microfauna changes are causing a pre-existing test to fail. The simplest fix would be to increase the detritus reference density from 0.5 to 2.0 g/L—this makes the microfauna less sensitive to short-term food spikes and more aligned with how they'd actually respond to sustained availability. I should verify what the default tank geometry is to understand the test's baseline conditions better, but the most targeted approach is probably just tuning that one parameter rather than reworking the entire population model. Updating the parameters and running the simulation again to see how these changes affect the microfauna behavior.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2330,"cache_read_input_tokens":154444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2330},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24615e58-d50c-4dac-86b6-bdc30a050fb6","uuid":"8ee56fa6-63bd-4df0-bf6f-3af556a0646e"}
